to hooterville no i thought it was kind of stupid myself i ought to knock you over that railing

00:18:38.660 --> 00:18:47.000
you shouldn't have said that betlow no why not because it scared me

00:18:48.440 --> 00:18:53.850
wait a second sit down that stupid train the only way to get to hooterville

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:59.830
no there's another way that's right you mean somebody can take me yeah

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:04.860
- I can take you.

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:06.580
- Now listen, old-timer.

00:19:06.580 --> 00:19:08.020
I didn't mean to yell at you.

00:19:08.020 --> 00:19:10.480
I know you're not in this with these other people.

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:13.080
I can see you're an intelligent man.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:14.760
- You can?

00:19:14.760 --> 00:19:16.550
- Now, I'm willing to pay you.

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:18.230
- No, no, I didn't ask for any money.

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:20.930
- I know you didn't, I'm forcing it on you.

00:19:20.930 --> 00:19:22.950
Come on, let's get going.

00:19:42.540 --> 00:19:43.420
What is this?

00:19:43.420 --> 00:19:45.240
I thought you were going to take me to Hooterville.

00:19:45.240 --> 00:19:46.290
I am.

00:19:46.290 --> 00:19:48.830
We'll never get there before tomorrow if you don't walk faster.

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:49.830
Walk?

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:52.370
There's no way you can get there outside the train.

00:19:52.370 --> 00:19:54.030
Well, why didn't you tell me?

00:19:54.030 --> 00:19:56.770
You didn't ask me.

00:19:56.770 --> 00:20:00.000
Let me have my money back.

00:20:03.360 --> 00:20:04.360
Indian giver.

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:15.950
Hiya, boys.

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:16.910
Hi, Sam.

00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:17.830
Kate.

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:20.430
Oh, Sam, I need your help.

00:20:20.430 --> 00:20:21.430
If we don't do something,

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:23.430
Charlie and Floyd are gonna lose their train.

00:20:23.430 --> 00:20:24.430
Well, I hate to tell you this,

00:20:24.430 --> 00:20:26.440
but they've already lost part of it.

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:28.440
I don't mean that.

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.000
Boys, you go on down.

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:32.710
and turn around and pick us up on the way back.

00:20:35.540 --> 00:20:48.370
- Deputy Sheriff? - I never was a Deputy Sheriff.

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:50.870
I was Marshal at the Fourth of July parade once.

00:20:50.870 --> 00:20:53.990
- That's close enough. Come on. - Well, what's this all about, Keith?

00:20:53.990 --> 00:20:56.550
Come on. I'll explain.

00:20:56.550 --> 00:20:59.510
Don't worry, girls. I'll handle Bedloe.

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.940
fighting the railroad's a man's job.

00:21:02.040 --> 00:21:02.740
Hey, everybody.

00:21:03.450 --> 00:21:04.710
Mr. Bedloe got away.

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:06.360
He's on his way to Hooterville.

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:07.060
I know.

00:21:07.160 --> 00:21:08.380
He found that old hand car

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:09.320
and got it on the track.

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:14.980
Ha, ha, ha, ha.

00:22:17.850 --> 00:22:19.210
As soon as he gets his wind back.

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:21.330
We're running for five miles.

00:22:22.050 --> 00:22:23.530
Are you going to be all right, Mr. Bedloe?

00:22:23.950 --> 00:22:24.950
I guess so.

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:27.330
Who are you?

00:22:27.730 --> 00:22:28.810
My name's Drucker.

00:22:28.910 --> 00:22:29.210
Hey!

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:32.290
Marshal Drucker from Hooterville.

00:22:32.290 --> 00:22:33.670
Marshal Drucker?

00:22:33.670 --> 00:22:34.950
He's a lawman.

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:36.490
Thought you ran a general store.

00:22:36.490 --> 00:22:39.090
Yeah, I do that too. Also published a newspaper.

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:40.630
Yeah, plus being the postmaster.

00:22:40.630 --> 00:22:42.010
And he's the mayor?

00:22:42.010 --> 00:22:43.710
Never mind, no man.

00:22:43.710 --> 00:22:47.470
No, if you're a marshal, arrest every one of these people.

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:48.510
What's the charge?

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:49.610
Stealing a train.

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:51.230
You mean Charlie and Floyd's train?

00:22:51.230 --> 00:22:52.730
That's my train!

00:22:52.730 --> 00:22:53.920
Oh!

00:22:53.920 --> 00:22:57.440
I'm the official representative of the CNFW railroads.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:22:58.800
Marshal Drucker, do your duty.

00:22:58.800 --> 00:23:00.000
Yeah, be quick about it.

00:23:00.730 --> 00:23:05.090
All right. Mr. Bedloe, I hereby serve you with these 27

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:08.350
summonses, complaints, subpoenas, and overdue bills.

00:23:08.350 --> 00:23:09.400
What for?

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:12.940
For the damages caused by your train during the past 20 years

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:16.280
and for supplies furnished and services rendered thereto

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:18.080
by the citizens of this valley

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:21.280
as named of the complaints, et cetera, in your hands.

00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:22.620
Why, that's ridiculous.

00:23:22.620 --> 00:23:24.980
Total's up to about $150,000.

00:23:24.980 --> 00:23:26.580
Now, I wouldn't call that ridiculous.

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:29.250
Maloney, what judge would honor that?

00:23:30.030 --> 00:23:32.030
Not you!

00:23:32.030 --> 00:23:34.030
I demand a jury trial!

00:23:34.030 --> 00:23:36.030
Boy, I don't think you could find 12 men around here

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:38.030
that ain't in that bunch of complainers.

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:40.030
Judge Drucker, isn't there some way

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:42.030
that this thing can be settled out of court?

00:23:42.030 --> 00:23:43.980
What do you mean, "out of court"?

00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:44.980
I got him right where I want him.

00:23:44.980 --> 00:23:45.980
Uncle Joe, please.

00:23:45.980 --> 00:23:47.980
Well, now, I can't, Kate.

00:23:47.980 --> 00:23:51.150
If Mr. Bedlow was to go quietly back to the city

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:55.160
and let Floyd and Charlie resume running their railroad,

00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:58.160
I might be inclined to show a little judicial leniency

00:23:58.160 --> 00:24:00.000
and postpone these cases

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:05.260
indefinitely oh don't do it let's have a big trial lots of people be good for

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:11.440
business Mr. Bedlow what do you say what can I say you got me over a barrel well

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:15.880
come on I'll back you into Hooterville it won't be as fast as that hand car but a

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:26.020
heap less time Kate you've pulled some beauties today but letting him go was the

00:24:26.020 --> 00:24:30.000
biggest blunder of all well you think so well he admitted himself

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:39.050
we had him over a barrel well i'm sorry i don't blame you kate but from now on leave these things

00:24:39.050 --> 00:24:48.150
up to me you know fighting the railroad's a man's job i'll remember uncle jim

00:25:30.760 --> 00:25:32.400
This has been a Filmways presentation.
